Line a #baking sheet{} with paper towels. Set the sliced @tomatoes{4}(sliced crosswise ¼-inch thick) on top in a single layer. In a small bowl, combine @salt{=1%tsp} and @black pepper{}. Use it to season the tomatoes evenly on both sides, cover with another paper towel and let sit, ~{15%minutes}, allowing the salt to draw moisture out of the tomatoes.

As the tomatoes sit, heat the oven to 400 degrees and set a rack in the middle of the oven. Working on a large sheet of parchment paper, unroll the @pie crust{1}(store-bought, thawed if frozen), pressing out any fold lines. Prick the inside with a fork every few inches, leaving a ½-inch border. Using a pastry brush, coat the center of the crust with @crème fraîche{3%tbsp}(or Greek yogurt), leaving the border unbrushed, then brush the border itself with a little extra crème fraîche so it browns evenly and doesn't dry out.

Working within the border, layer the tomatoes and @red onion{¼}(very thinly sliced) on top of the tart, allowing them to overlap slightly. Transfer to a sheet pan and bake, rotating halfway through, until crust is golden brown and crisp, ~{25-30%minutes}.

Sprinkle tomatoes with @?Aleppo pepper{} or @?red-pepper flakes{} if using. Season @fresh ricotta{¾ cup} with a pinch of #salt{} and pepper, then dollop over the tart. Thin @pesto{(store-bought or homemade)} with @extra-virgin olive oil{} until it reaches the proper consistency for drizzling; drizzle on top of tart to taste. Top with @?basil leaves{} if using, and serve warm or at room temperature.
